\ Select the complete month weaving with bowl 4 and seal 32.
\ Each month occurs exactly as many times as its chosen length.
\ The first occurrence of month 1 precedes that of month 2, etc.
\ The last occurrence of month 1 also precedes that of month 2, etc.
\ These are two independent restrictions; BOTH must be enforced.
\
\ Exact completion recurrence:
\ F(r) = sum over eligible month i of F(r with r[i] reduced by one).
\ F(0,...,0)=1. A candidate is eligible when:
\   1. its remaining count is positive;
\   2. it has already appeared, or is the next never-seen month;
\   3. if this is its last occurrence, its ordinal is the smallest
\      month which has not yet finished.
\ Thus the first and last occurrences each follow 1..m.
\
\ A memo table keyed by the exact vector of remaining counts avoids
\ recomputing the same suffix. Strings have separators between counts,
\ so states never collide. All counts are arbitrary-precision strings.
\ Unranking visits candidates 1..m in order and skips complete blocks.
\ The selected path is unranked in full. Tests may retain the whole
\ weaving, while ordinary date conversion stores only the requested
\ day's month identity and occurrence, without allocating every day.
\
\ LIMITATION: sparse memoization can still use prohibitive memory for
\ 47 months and long years. A strict 4096-state limit causes an explicit
\ failure instead of a fabricated date or unbounded memory growth.
\ This is an exact reference implementation, not an optimized
\ large-instance release. CAL compilation is unverified.

A woven month state is a thing with
  a number called ordinal,
  a number called total days,
  a number called remaining days.

A woven day record is a thing with
  a number called month ordinal.

A weave cached result is a thing with
  a string called signature,
  a wide quantity called completions.

The woven month states are some woven month states.
The chosen woven days are some woven day records.
The weave completion cache is some weave cached results.

The examined woven month state is a woven month state.
The examined woven day record is a woven day record.
The matched weave result is a weave cached result.

A weaving selection has
  a number called days remaining,
  a number called already started,
  a number called already finished,
  a number called candidate ordinal,
  a number called candidate legal,
  a number called selected ordinal,
  a number called position,
  a number called target position,
  a number called preserve full weaving,
  a number called selection success,
  a number called recursion depth,
  a number called memo entry count,
  a number called memo entry limit,
  a number called resource limit reached,
  a wide quantity called total completions,
  a wide quantity called current rank,
  a string called state signature.

The weaving selection is a weaving selection.
The woven requested month ordinal is a number.
The woven requested month day is a number.

\ Dedicated scalar aliases preserve per-call local storage in the
\ recursive completion accumulator. Do not replace with global scratch:
\ nested recursion must not overwrite the caller's running sum.
A weavesum is a wide quantity.
A weavechild is a wide quantity.
A weaveindex is a number.

\ Read month lengths into independent state records without modifying
\ the selected year lengths. Each list has month indices 1..m.
To prepare the woven month states:
  Destroy the woven month states.
  Put 0 into the weaving selection's days remaining.
  Put 1 into a month ordinal number.
  Put the chosen month lengths' first into an examined month length record.
  Loop.
    If the examined month length record is nil, break.
    Allocate memory for a woven month state.
    Put the month ordinal number into the woven month state's ordinal.
    Put the examined month length record's month length days into the woven month state's total days.
    Put the woven month state's total days into the woven month state's remaining days.
    Append the woven month state to the woven month states.
    Add the woven month state's total days to the weaving selection's days remaining.
    Add 1 to the month ordinal number.
    Put the examined month length record's next into the examined month length record.
  Repeat.

\ Lookup always uses a month ordinal, never a name index.
To find the woven month state given a number:
  Put the woven month states' first into the examined woven month state.
  Loop.
    If the examined woven month state is nil, break.
    If the examined woven month state's ordinal is the number, break.
    Put the examined woven month state's next into the examined woven month state.
  Repeat.

\ Every started month is a prefix 1..f. Every finished month is 1..d.
\ These facts follow from the prescribed first and last order.
To inspect the woven month status:
  Put 0 into the weaving selection's already started.
  Put 0 into the weaving selection's already finished.
  Put the woven month states' first into the examined woven month state.
  Loop.
    If the examined woven month state is nil, break.
    If the examined woven month state's remaining days is less than the examined woven month state's total days,
      add 1 to the weaving selection's already started.
    If the examined woven month state's remaining days is 0,
      add 1 to the weaving selection's already finished.
    Put the examined woven month state's next into the examined woven month state.
  Repeat.

\ A first appearance cannot jump over an unstarted month. A last
\ appearance cannot finish a month before all earlier ones finish.
To inspect candidate woven months given a number:
  Put 0 into the weaving selection's candidate legal.
  Find the woven month state given the number.
  If the examined woven month state is nil, exit.
  If the examined woven month state's remaining days is 0, exit.
  Inspect the woven month status.
  Find the woven month state given the number.
  Put the weaving selection's already started into an allowed first number.
  Add 1 to the allowed first number.
  If the number is greater than the allowed first number, exit.
  If the examined woven month state's remaining days is 1,
    check eligible candidate final occurrence given the number; exit.
  Put 1 into the weaving selection's candidate legal.

To check eligible candidate final occurrence given a number:
  Put the weaving selection's already finished into an allowed last number.
  Add 1 to the allowed last number.
  If the number is the allowed last number,
    put 1 into the weaving selection's candidate legal.

\ The signature is lossless even when a month has 123 remaining days.
To make the weaving state signature:
  Put "" into the weaving selection's state signature.
  Put the woven month states' first into the examined woven month state.
  Loop.
    If the examined woven month state is nil, break.
    Convert the examined woven month state's remaining days to a remaining text string.
    Append the remaining text string to the weaving selection's state signature.
    Append "/" to the weaving selection's state signature.
    Put the examined woven month state's next into the examined woven month state.
  Repeat.

To find a weave cached result given a string:
  Put the weave completion cache's first into the matched weave result.
  Loop.
    If the matched weave result is nil, break.
    If the matched weave result's signature is the string, break.
    Put the matched weave result's next into the matched weave result.
  Repeat.

To save weaving completions given a string and a wide quantity:
  If the weaving selection's memo entry count is not less than the weaving selection's memo entry limit,
    put 1 into the weaving selection's resource limit reached; exit.
  Allocate memory for a weave cached result.
  Put the string into the weave cached result's signature.
  Put the wide quantity into the weave cached result's completions.
  Prepend the weave cached result to the weave completion cache.
  Add 1 to the weaving selection's memo entry count.

\ Recursion uses local candidate number and local sum. The mutable
\ remaining-count vector is always restored before moving to the
\ next candidate. Cached zero counts are legitimate as well.
\\ Keep the call stack bounded even if few memo entries have been
\\ allocated. All exits from the recursive body come back here, so the
\\ recursion depth is restored on success and on resource failure.
To count weaving completions into a wide quantity:
  If the weaving selection's resource limit reached is 1,
    put "0" into the wide quantity; exit.
  If the weaving selection's recursion depth is not less than 256,
    put 1 into the weaving selection's resource limit reached;
    put "0" into the wide quantity; exit.
  Add 1 to the weaving selection's recursion depth.
  Resolve weaving completions into the wide quantity.
  Subtract 1 from the weaving selection's recursion depth.

To resolve weaving completions into a wide quantity:
  If the weaving selection's resource limit reached is 1,
    put "0" into the wide quantity; exit.
  If the weaving selection's days remaining is 0,
    put "1" into the wide quantity; exit.
  Count ordered last weaving suffix completions.
  If the started weaving suffix's available is 1,
    put the started weaving suffix's completions into the wide quantity; exit.
  Count a short weaving suffix.
  If the short weaving suffix's completed is 1,
    put the short weaving suffix's completions into the wide quantity; exit.
  Make the weaving state signature.
  Put the weaving selection's state signature into a saved signature string.
  Find a weave cached result given the saved signature string.
  If the matched weave result is not nil,
    put the matched weave result's completions into the wide quantity; exit.
  Put "0" into a weavesum.
  Put 1 into a weaveindex.
  Loop.
    If the weaveindex is greater than the selected month count, break.
    Count eligible candidate weaving suffix given the weaveindex into a weavechild.
    Add the weavechild to the weavesum.
    Add 1 to the weaveindex.
  Repeat.
  If the weaving selection's resource limit reached is 1,
    put "0" into the wide quantity; exit.
  Save weaving completions given the saved signature string and the weavesum.
  If the weaving selection's resource limit reached is 1,
    put "0" into the wide quantity; exit.
  Put the weavesum into the wide quantity.

\ Decrease the candidate's count, ask recursively, then restore both
\ its remaining count and the total. Never use a pointer retained from
\ the recursive call, because its lookup state may have changed.
To count eligible candidate weaving suffix given a number into a wide quantity:
  Put "0" into the wide quantity.
  Inspect candidate woven months given the number.
  If the weaving selection's candidate legal is not 1, exit.
  Find the woven month state given the number.
  Subtract 1 from the examined woven month state's remaining days.
  Subtract 1 from the weaving selection's days remaining.
  Count weaving completions into the wide quantity.
  Find the woven month state given the number.
  Add 1 to the examined woven month state's remaining days.
  Add 1 to the weaving selection's days remaining.

\ Prepare a new exact counting session from chosen month lengths.
To count all possible month weavings:
  Prepare the woven month states.
  Destroy the weave completion cache.
  Put 0 into the weaving selection's selection success.
  Put 0 into the weaving selection's resource limit reached.
  Put 0 into the weaving selection's memo entry count.
  Put 0 into the weaving selection's recursion depth.
  Put 4096 into the weaving selection's memo entry limit.
  Put 1 into the weaving selection's preserve full weaving.
  Count weaving completions into the weaving selection's total completions.
  If the weaving selection's resource limit reached is 1,
    put "0" into the weaving selection's total completions.

To store the accepted woven day:
  If the weaving selection's preserve full weaving is 0, exit.
  Allocate memory for a woven day record.
  Put the weaving selection's selected ordinal into the woven day record's month ordinal.
  Append the woven day record to the chosen woven days.

\ The candidate's suffix block has already been counted without
\ consuming a permanent day. Compare that block with the rank.
To consider the next woven candidate given a number and a wide quantity:
  If the wide quantity is "0", exit.
  If the wide quantity is numerically smaller than the weaving selection's current rank,
    subtract the wide quantity from the weaving selection's current rank; exit.
  Put the number into the weaving selection's selected ordinal.
  Find the woven month state given the number.
  Subtract 1 from the examined woven month state's remaining days.
  Subtract 1 from the weaving selection's days remaining.
  Add 1 to the weaving selection's position.
  Store the accepted woven day.
  If the weaving selection's position is the weaving selection's target position,
    remember requested woven position.
  
To remember requested woven position:
  Put the weaving selection's selected ordinal into the woven requested month ordinal.
  Find the woven month state given the woven requested month ordinal.
  Put the examined woven month state's total days into the woven requested month day.
  Subtract the examined woven month state's remaining days from the woven requested month day.

\ Each lexicographic candidate is counted before any is selected.
To choose the next woven day:
  Put 0 into the weaving selection's selected ordinal.
  Put 1 into a candidate ordinal number.
  Loop.
    If the candidate ordinal number is greater than the selected month count, break.
    Count eligible candidate weaving suffix given the candidate ordinal number into a block wide quantity.
    Consider the next woven candidate given the candidate ordinal number and the block wide quantity.
    If the weaving selection's selected ordinal is greater than 0, break.
    Add 1 to the candidate ordinal number.
  Repeat.

\ The caller supplies a one-based rank of the full weaving sequence.
\ Unranking consumes remaining counts. Rebuild them from the
\ original month lengths BEFORE every requested rank, even after
\ a previous full or streaming selection. The exact memo cache may
\ remain because the original month lengths have not changed.
To select a weaving selection from a wide quantity:
  Put 0 into the weaving selection's selection success.
  Destroy the chosen woven days.
  Put 0 into the woven requested month ordinal.
  Put 0 into the woven requested month day.
  If the weaving selection's resource limit reached is 1, exit.
  If the wide quantity is "0", exit.
  If the weaving selection's total completions is numerically smaller than the wide quantity, exit.
  Prepare the woven month states.
  Put the wide quantity into the weaving selection's current rank.
  Put 0 into the weaving selection's position.
  Loop.
    If the weaving selection's days remaining is 0, break.
    Choose the next woven day.
    If the weaving selection's selected ordinal is 0, exit.
  Repeat.
  Put 1 into the weaving selection's selection success.

\ All questions for month structure use (c, first day of year).
\ Month lengths and their count must already have been selected.
To choose the canonical month weaving:
  Count all possible month weavings.
  If the weaving selection's total completions is "0", exit.
  Put 0 into the weaving selection's preserve full weaving.
  Prepare the year structure sauce.
  Put 4 into the seal inquiry's bowl identity.
  Put 32 into the seal inquiry's seal number.
  Begin a seal inquiry.
  Determine canonical equal selection given the weaving selection's total completions.
  Select a weaving selection from the seal inquiry's selected quantity.
